# Product

The person doing the work comes first. A change earns its place when it shortens that work or removes a mistake that costs the organisation. A screen, field, or step that does not change a decision or a task stays out.

When a clearer path for the person and a tidier structure disagree, take the clearer path, using the interaction the rest of the product already uses.

Then the code. Copy the exemplar for that kind of code. Reuse a function or pattern that already exists. Extract a new shared piece when a second caller needs the same behaviour. Write the smallest change that does the job, short enough to read straight through.

Security is part of that smallest change. Validate input, escape output, and keep authorisation on the server. Do not invent a second way to do something the application already does safely. Do not restyle the application into a different framework's habits.

User-facing sentences follow `writing-style.mdc`. Forms follow `form-rendering.mdc`. A blueprint follows `application-blueprints.mdc`. The file to copy for each kind of code is in `exemplars.mdc`.
